{
  "answer": "The retained passages support that cryptocurrency can be property held on trust, but ownership and trust rights depend on the particular terms, consent and party relationship. The 2023 decision rejected a trust under the 2018 terms while preserving the non-consenting-customer exception. The 2025 decision recognised pooled beneficial interests for non-consenting customers, distinguished ETD holders’ breach-of-trust claims and rejected Samara’s claimed trust against Gatecoin. These conclusions do not establish that a Product Provider is a trustee or has fulfilled paragraph 10 of 26EC22. Transfer requires an applicable legal proposition, a justified comparison of relationships and actual product facts. Later treatment remains unresolved, and the scenario-seven inconsistency must remain open.",
  "claims": [
    {
      "inference": "Text: capacity to be trust property is distinct from creation of a trust. The court’s conclusion concerning customers governed by the 2018 terms cannot be extended to all cryptocurrency holders.",
      "missing_premises": [
        "The terms governing the particular customer and evidence of assent.",
        "The complete reasoning needed to determine the precise scope of the holding."
      ],
      "proposition": "The 2023 judgment recognised cryptocurrency as property capable of trust but found no trust intention under the 2018 terms, preserving the non-consenting-customer exception.",
      "status": "Supported by retained judgment passage; broader application unresolved",
      "support": [
        {
          "quote": "Cryptocurrency is “property” and is capable of being held on trust",
          "unit_id": "gatecoin-2023.conclusion-82"
        },
        {
          "quote": "However, the terms of the 2018 T&C show that there was no certainty of intention to create a trust over the cryptocurrencies held by Gatecoin",
          "unit_id": "gatecoin-2023.conclusion-82"
        },
        {
          "quote": "Except the Non-Consenting Customers, the 2018 T&C applied to, and governed the relationship between all the Customers",
          "unit_id": "gatecoin-2023.conclusion-82"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "inference": "Text attribution: the 2023 passage quotes principles summarised by Briggs J in Re Lehman Brothers. Deduction: labels and intermediary status alone do not establish beneficial ownership; the relevant bilateral relationship must be assessed objectively.",
      "missing_premises": [
        "The actual agreements, commercial arrangements and property concerned.",
        "A checked account of how the quoted principles were applied in the full reasons."
      ],
      "proposition": "Words such as trust, custody and ownership, and the existence of an agency or brokerage relationship, are relevant but not conclusive.",
      "status": "Supported as quoted reasoning, not an independently verified general ratio",
      "support": [
        {
          "quote": "The words used by the parties such as ‘trust’, ‘custody’, ‘belonging’, ‘ownership’, ‘title’, may be persuasive, but they are not conclusive in favour of the recognition of B’s proprietary interest in the property, if the terms of the agreement or relationship, viewed objectively, compel a different conclusion.",
          "unit_id": "gatecoin-2023.terms-and-intention"
        },
        {
          "quote": "The identification of a relationship in which A is B’s agent or broker is not conclusive of a conclusion that A is, in relation to the property, B’s trustee, although it may be a pointer towards that conclusion.",
          "unit_id": "gatecoin-2023.terms-and-intention"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "inference": "Text: classification depends on platform access, instruction timing and contrary evidence. Deduction: a post-change ledger entry alone does not prove customer consent to the changed terms.",
      "missing_premises": [
        "Customer-specific evidence of platform access, instructions and timing.",
        "The full definition of non-consenting customers and applicable evidential procedures."
      ],
      "proposition": "The 2025 reasoning treats scenarios one to four as presumptively non-consenting and scenarios five and six as presumptively outside that category, subject to the specified evidence.",
      "status": "Supported by retained paragraph 8; not a complete classifier",
      "support": [
        {
          "quote": "The customers should be regarded as NCCs unless there is evidence to show that they have accessed or used the Platform to effect the transactions",
          "unit_id": "gatecoin-2025.classification-8"
        },
        {
          "quote": "The transactions described in the 5th to 6th scenarios on their face required the customers to access or use the Platform to effect them and, prima facie, the customers fall outside the definition of NCC.",
          "unit_id": "gatecoin-2025.classification-8"
        },
        {
          "quote": "However, if the customers are able to prove that they gave the instruction to Gatecoin before the Relevant Date or without using the Platform, they should be regarded as NCCs.",
          "unit_id": "gatecoin-2025.classification-8"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "inference": "Text: the disposition assigns different rights to different claimants and relationships. Deduction: neither an account entry nor a proprietary claim involving another party determines a trust against Gatecoin.",
      "missing_premises": [
        "The complete reasoning and underlying agreements for each distinct relationship.",
        "The claimant’s proved classification and the applicable pool, shortfall and allocation facts."
      ],
      "proposition": "The 2025 judgment distinguishes NCC pooled beneficial ownership, ETD breach-of-trust claims, Samara’s unsecured claim against Gatecoin and assets recorded in BlueFire’s accounts.",
      "status": "Supported by retained disposition and summary",
      "support": [
        {
          "quote": "Each NCC is a beneficial tenant-in-common in a pool of cryptocurrency and fiat currency.",
          "unit_id": "gatecoin-2025.disposition-95"
        },
        {
          "quote": "ETD represents a cause of action by ETD Holder against Gatecoin for breach of trust.",
          "unit_id": "gatecoin-2025.disposition-95"
        },
        {
          "quote": "Gatecoin does not hold the Subject Bitcoins or the Specified Account on trust for Mr Samara.",
          "unit_id": "gatecoin-2025.disposition-95"
        },
        {
          "quote": "Mr Samara is only an unsecured creditor to the extent of his claim less the amount he already received through execution of the HCA Judgment.",
          "unit_id": "gatecoin-2025.disposition-95"
        },
        {
          "quote": "The Currencies recorded in BlueFire’s accounts are assets of Gatecoin.",
          "unit_id": "gatecoin-2025.earlier-decision"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "inference": "Comparison of text: paragraph 8 rejects scenario seven as nonexistent, whereas paragraph 95 includes it within an evidential exception. The annexure supplies additional classifications and a deadline but does not expressly resolve that conflict. No priority rule or correction is assumed.",
      "missing_premises": [
        "Reconciliation of the full reasons, annexure and operative order.",
        "Any authoritative clarification or correction of scenario seven."
      ],
      "proposition": "The retained 2025 passages do not support an unqualified executable classification for scenario seven.",
      "status": "Unresolved source interpretation",
      "support": [
        {
          "quote": "The transactions described in the 7th scenario did not exist and the customers should not be regarded as NCC.",
          "unit_id": "gatecoin-2025.classification-8"
        },
        {
          "quote": "The 5th to 7th scenarios prima facie fall outside the definition of NCC but if the customers can prove that they gave the relevant instructions to Gatecoin before the Relevant Date or without using the Platform, they should be regarded as NCCs.",
          "unit_id": "gatecoin-2025.disposition-95"
        },
        {
          "quote": "Any Customer who claims to have transactions enumerated in paragraphs 1.2(b) shall notify the Liquidators and provide evidence to the Liquidators in support of such a claim within 21 days from the date hereof.",
          "unit_id": "gatecoin-2025.annexure-issue1"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "inference": "Deduction: Gatecoin’s relationship-specific trust analysis may inform examination of product ownership, but regulatory responsibility and proprietary title answer different questions. Judgment: transfer requires an explicit legal and factual analogy; Product Provider status alone supplies neither trusteeship nor fulfillment.",
      "missing_premises": [
        "Applicability of the retained 26EC22 edition to the entity, product, activity and assessment period, including conditions and amendments.",
        "A verified transferable proposition from the full judgments, its authority and subsequent treatment.",
        "Product constitutive documents, customer consent, custody and outsourcing agreements, governing law, settlement finality and legal versus beneficial ownership.",
        "A reasoned comparison of the relevant parties and rights with Gatecoin’s exchange-insolvency relationships.",
        "An applicable fulfillment standard and actual evidence of retained authority, supervisory conduct, operational soundness and proper ownership records."
      ],
      "proposition": "Applying Gatecoin to a Product Provider requires a justified transfer; paragraph 10 and footnote 1 retain responsibility despite outsourcing but do not themselves establish a trust or a sufficient discharge test.",
      "status": "Source-grounded deduction; application and fulfillment not established",
      "support": [
        {
          "quote": "Product Providers should remain and be ultimately responsible for the management and operational soundness of the tokenisation arrangement adopted and record keeping of ownership, regardless of any outsourcing arrangement1.",
          "unit_id": "d3b41d4d7e61c.u38.3432"
        },
        {
          "quote": "the Product Provider should remain fully responsible, despite any outsourcing arrangement such as those envisaged under 6.8 of the Code on Unit Trusts and Mutual Funds.",
          "unit_id": "d3b41d4d7e61c.u85.7210"
        },
        {
          "quote": "Product Providers should ensure that proper records of token holders’ ownership interests in the product are maintained and the tokenisation arrangement is operationally compatible with service providers involved2.",
          "unit_id": "d3b41d4d7e61c.u41.3664"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "legal_correctness_established": false,
  "question_id": "gatecoin",
  "rival_readings": [
    "Scenario seven: paragraph 8 supports exclusion because the transactions did not exist; paragraph 95 facially permits NCC status on proof of earlier or off-platform instructions. Neither reading is silently preferred.",
    "Transfer: Gatecoin supports investigating objectively established ownership relationships; a stronger reading that all Product Providers hold assets on trust is unsupported.",
    "Paragraph 10 can be read principally as retaining ultimate responsibility, or in conjunction with surrounding requirements as requiring substantive performance. The supplied passages do not establish that allocation, an isolated supervisory act or a satisfactory outcome alone is sufficient."
  ],
  "unresolved": [
    "The 2025 judgment expressly records the 2023 conclusions, but complete subsequent treatment of both judgments and their present authoritative force remain unverified.",
    "Only selected case passages are supplied; candidate statements that full reasons were checked do not establish that verification here.",
    "The case manifests repeat a source hash that differs from the distinct acquisition hashes; provenance needs reconciliation before reliance on those manifests.",
    "Rendered DOCX fidelity, the scenario-seven tension and complete customer-classification rules remain unverified.",
    "No actual Product Provider facts or applicable discharge standard establish compliance. Supplied parser, graph and serialization results do not establish legal truth."
  ]
